  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 300 PM CDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\nShowers and thunderstorms have cleared out of the area as of\nearly this afternoon, but considerable cloud cover remains, esp.\nfor areas S of I-80. As a result, temperatures remain quite cool\nfor early August - though not as cool as yesterday - in the \nupper 60s to middle 70s. Winds aren't nearly as breezy as\nyesterday, either. Clouds will continue the decreasing trend and\nallow for a late day run into the 70s to lower 80s, which\ncombined with continued light winds, should make for a very\npleasant late-summer evening.\n\nThe clearing skies, light winds, and recent rainfall will create\nfavorable conditions for the development of fog late tonight and\ninto Thursday morning, particularly right around dawn. Fog will\nbe possible just about anywhere, but models are hitting areas\nalong/S of Platte River down to around the KS/NE state line the\nhardest. General pattern recognition and recent model trends do\nsupport areas of dense fog and very low visibilities, so a\nheadline may be warranted at some point this evening or\novernight. For now, have hit the fog a bit harder in the grids\nand continued mention in the most recent Hazardous Weather\nOutlook (HWO). Any fog should be rather shallow and fairly quick\nto mix out between 8-10AM Thursday morning.\n\nThe main weather story heading into the weekend will be the\nreturn of hot and humid conditions as a dome of upper level \nhigh pressure over the Desert SW builds/expands northeastward\nover the Central Plains. This will force the primary band of\nwesterlies to retreat well N of the region for several days and\nleave the region under mostly clear skies and persistent\nsoutherly low level flow. Highs will build into the lower 90s to\nlower 100s over the weekend, warmest in our far SW zones. So no\nreason why dew points wouldn't return back into at least the mid\n60s to lower 70s, and wouldn't rule out some mid to upper 70s in\ntypically favored areas along/E of Hwy 281. Heat indices will\nrespond accordingly into the upper 90s to lower 100s, though are\ncurrently forecast to fall just shy of Heat Advisory criteria of\n105F. With that said, any significant upward trend in forecast\npeak heat indices could warrant one when considering the heat\nwill be around for 3-4 days. On the other hand, it's early\nAugust and we're well acclimated to heat & humidity at this\npoint, so would want to see peak forecast values more than just\n105-106F. There should be some Srly breezes to help out too.\n\nForecast appears pretty quiet in terms of rain chances next\nseveral days. As always in these types of patterns, will need to\nwatch for some \"sneaky\" weak waves rounding the crest of the\nridge nearby or just to the N...but these would tend to favor\nthe overnight hours and areas mainly N of I-80.\n\nThere is decent model agreement and run-to-run consistency\namongst ensembles (and even some deterministic runs) to feel\npretty confident about a notable pattern change arriving for the\nmiddle to later portions of next week. In fact, the most recent\n50-member 12Z EPS shows a broad swath of positive 5-day\nprecipitation anomalies across the central Plains in the 12-17th\ntime frame. The upper ridge is forecast to flatten and reorient\nmore into the Deep South during this time frame. This would\nallow for frequent rain chances from weak waves within\nincreasingly fast zonal upper flow and/or from convection\nworking off the High Plains. Either way, the orientation of the\nupper ridge should allow for some mid-upper level monsoonal \nmoisture to work in from the SW and aid in precipitation.",
